### **Cómo Obtener AzureRoleDefinitionId**

```powershell
# Método 1: Extraer con el script
.\PIM-SecureAssignment-ARM.ps1 -ExtractRoles -SupportAzureRBAC -ExportRolesCsv

# Método 2: Comando directo
Get-AzRoleDefinition | Where-Object { $_.Name -like "*Contributor*" } |
    Select-Object Name, Id | Format-Table

# Método 3: Buscar rol específico por nombre
$role = Get-AzRoleDefinition -Name "Contributor"
Write-Host "AzureRoleDefinitionId: $($role.Id)"

# Método 4: Listar roles custom
Get-AzRoleDefinition | Where-Object { $_.IsCustom -eq $true } |
    Select-Object Name, Id, Description | Format-Table
```

### **Cómo Obtener AzureScope**

```powershell
# Obtener ID de suscripción actual
$sub = Get-AzContext
Write-Host "Scope suscripción: /subscriptions/$($sub.Subscription.Id)"

# Obtener scope de un resource group
$rg = Get-AzResourceGroup -Name "rg-production"
Write-Host "Scope RG: $($rg.ResourceId)"

# Obtener scope de una VM específica
$vm = Get-AzVM -ResourceGroupName "rg-compute" -Name "vm-web-01"
Write-Host "Scope VM: $($vm.Id)"

# Obtener scope de un storage account
$storage = Get-AzStorageAccount -ResourceGroupName "rg-data" -Name "stdatalake01"
Write-Host "Scope Storage: $($storage.Id)"

# Listar todos los resource groups en formato CSV
Get-AzResourceGroup | Select-Object ResourceGroupName, 
    @{N='Scope';E={$_.ResourceId}} | 
    Export-Csv "resource_groups_scopes.csv"
```

